WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.884
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.210
JENNIFER PERSON: So why are we
talking about managing payments

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.170
today?

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:17.410
Well, a lot of apps require you
to be able to exchange money,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.140
especially because that's
probably the primary way

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.600
that you are making
profit off of an app.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.190
It would be nice if we
could just do it out

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:26.440
of the goodness of our hearts.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.390
But it is important to be able
to break even or make a profit.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:33.790
So a lot of different
applications

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.180
require that ability.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.380
So for instance,
mobile commerce apps--

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.210
there are all sorts
of stores, seem

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.220
to have their own
applications right now.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.340
And there's a rich
on-demand economy.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.960
You can get anything delivered
to your door within 24 hours,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.965
or ridesharing, et cetera.

00:00:51.965 --> 00:00:53.590
And there's also
software as a service,

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.930
like subscription
for billing software,

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:57.880
Cloud services, et cetera.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.730
We are most focused on
these first two bullets

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:04.300
because if you're familiar with
how apps work in the App Store,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.720
when it comes to goods
that are digital,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.170
that is managed differently.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.795
So in this case, we're
talking about tangible goods.

00:01:12.795 --> 00:01:14.170
Specifically,
we're talking about

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.140
a hypothetical
online flower store

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.690
that Susan and I
are going to open.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.120
And it is going to be
called Mobile Florist.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.492
And Susan's going to tell you a
little bit more about our app.

00:01:25.492 --> 00:01:26.700
SUSAN GOLDBLATT: Thanks, Jen.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.310
Yeah, so we've been building all
this great flower application.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.893
I'm very excited about it.

00:01:31.893 --> 00:01:33.310
The way that this
will work-- it's

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.890
a four-step process, like
many things in our talk today.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.460
And you'll order
flowers via the app.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.130
So figure out which flowers
you want and select them.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.510
And then, step 2 will be
scheduling a delivery.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:49.270
So when will our person come and
deliver these flowers to you?

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.400
Then, the third step
is making a payment

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.980
via the app-- so actually
using your credit card

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.830
to charge the customer,
that that'll be most of what

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.197
we talk about during this talk.

00:01:59.197 --> 00:02:01.530
And then, step 4 will be
getting your flowers delivered.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.440
So how do you actually
get the services?

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.818
So that's how we'll build
out the business model.

00:02:07.818 --> 00:02:09.610
And with that in mind,
I'll go into talking

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:13.190
a little bit about how we'll
be building the application.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.610
So there's four steps
to this process.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.840
And we'll do an
authentication process.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.550
So how does a user log in
and access their information?

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.610
How do we create
them in Firebase?

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:25.850
How do we schedule things?

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.732
So store the information.

00:02:27.732 --> 00:02:30.190
And then finally, how are we
going to manage those customer

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:31.330
payments?

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.930
And then, we'll follow
up with how might we

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.890
handle a refund in
the application--

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.145
so just a little bit more
of a use case for refunds.

00:02:41.145 --> 00:02:42.520
So we know that
managing payments

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.313
can be kind of intimidating.

00:02:44.313 --> 00:02:46.730
This is the part where you
actually charge your customers.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.470
And so if you do it wrong,
you can do it really wrong.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.790
And no one wants that.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:52.750
It erodes user trust.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.750
So we're going to talk about
how you can handle payments

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.210
in a way that's safe and
seamless for your customers.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.155
And we'll use two technologies,
Stripe and Firebase.

00:03:02.155 --> 00:03:03.530
So I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.800
about why we're using Stripe.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.000
First, I'll explain what Stripe
is in case we don't know.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.110
It's a payment processor.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.910
And so that means
that it handles

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.080
charging the credit cards.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.930
And this can be really
helpful because we

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.780
don't want to be in the
business of handling credit card

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.950
information in our app.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.660
We want to focus
on flower delivery

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.860
because that's
what we care about.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.700
And so we want to take the
process of actually making

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.040
payments, and handling credit
card information, and all

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.990
of the security
concerns-- like what

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:34.740
if someone is trying
to use an old card?

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.800
How do we handle that?

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.630
And Stripe and other
payment processors

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:38.900
do a really good job at this.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.560
So for us, it makes
the most sense

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.990
to use a payment
processor to handle

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:48.430
that type of information, and
security, and convenience.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.040
So we're going to
use Stripe here.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.530
But you definitely don't
have to use Stripe.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.245
We really only picked it
because we know the Stripe API,

00:03:55.245 --> 00:03:56.370
and we're familiar with it.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.620
But you can use any payment
processor that you want.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.470
So that's why we're
going to use Stripe.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.637
And I'll pass it off to
Jenn to explain a little bit

00:04:03.637 --> 00:04:04.930
about why we're using Firebase.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.055
JENNIFER PERSON: Thank you.

00:04:06.055 --> 00:04:07.758
So there are actually
several reasons

00:04:07.758 --> 00:04:09.300
that we're going to
be using Firebase

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.300
because we are using different
products along different steps.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.672
So I'm going to break it down
by each step that we're taking.

00:04:16.672 --> 00:04:18.089
Here are the
Firebase technologies

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:19.769
that we're going
to be using today--

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.019
Firebase Authentication,
Cloud Firestore,

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:25.420
and Cloud Functions
for Firebase.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.460
Going back to our
four steps-- yes,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.920
this is going to
come up quite a bit--

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.760
our first step is going to
be authentication, in order

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.070
for us to know
who is going to be

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.020
paying, where we're
sending the flowers,

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.272
storing that information.

00:04:39.272 --> 00:04:41.230
So when the user comes
back to the application,

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.970
they still have all that.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:48.120
We need a way to verify
who our users are.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.120
But we know that
auth is very complex,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.170
though it is essential.

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:55.430
If you've ever rolled your own
auth, you already know this.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.380
One option that
people use a lot is

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:00.890
federated identity providers,
like Google, or Facebook,

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:01.652
or Twitter.

00:05:01.652 --> 00:05:03.110
And these are really
great options.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.810
They definitely eliminate
some of the difficult work

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.350
of setting up auth.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.260
But at the same time, there
are still some challenges.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.100
So for instance, what
if your customers don't

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.590
want to use that account
to sign into your app,

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:17.510
or they just don't have one.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:20.090
Or if you want to give them
more than one option, how

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.890
are you going to make
sure that a user who

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.280
signs in with Google, and a
user who signs in with Facebook

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.200
still have the
same type of user,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.840
the same type of information?

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.230
So another problem is, what
if someone accidentally

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:35.320
logs in with one provider
one day and another the next?

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.750
How are you going to
handle those situations?

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.260
Fortunately, there's
Firebase Auth.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:45.520
So Firebase Auth supports
all these methods

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.290
of authentication and more--

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.920
so email and password, Google,
Facebook, Twitter, GitHub,

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.940
Anonymous, which is very useful.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.060
So for instance,
when a user first

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.820
goes into our application,
it's not the best experience

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:01.420
to right out of the box
make them need to sign in.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.930
They aren't invested
yet, and that can really

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.400
ruin the experience.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.560
So allowing them
to explore the app

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.960
and then log in once
they're ready to schedule

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.140
can give you a much
better experience.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.640
At the same time, you still
want to protect your information

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:20.110
with security rules and be able
to identify who your users are.

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.660
Anonymous Auth allows
you to do that.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.250
When a user first
comes into the app,

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.800
you can log them in anonymously.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.110
And then every time they
return to the application,

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:32.140
it is still the same user
with the same user ID.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.900
And even better, when
it is time for that user

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.900
then to schedule something,
and they choose the way

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.180
that they're going to
log in, it actually

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:43.480
links automatically
that anonymous user

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:47.770
with the email and password
or federated identity provider

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.090
that they sign in with--

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:51.980
so at least, for a very
seamless experience.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.000
And if you're interested in a
UI that looks a little like this

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.530
one-- you'll actually see
another example of it further

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:00.250
in the talk--

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:04.360
this is an example of Firebase
UI, which is an open source

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.380
project on GitHub that allows
you to build some UI out

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.850
of the box for different
Firebase products,

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:12.220
including Auth.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.740
And it really is as
easy to add a provider

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:17.120
as it is to add a
single line of code.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.420
And I know that gets
thrown around a lot.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:22.640
But in this case, it
really is that simple.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.840
So yeah, that's why
we're using Firebase

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.630
Auth for authentication.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.950
Our next step is going
to be scheduling.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:32.950
And we need to store that
scheduling information

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:37.990
somewhere, so that our customers
can see their scheduling

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:38.740
information.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.240
We need to be able
to see all of it,

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.940
so we can figure out
where to send the flowers.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:47.950
So in this case, we are going
to use Cloud Firestore to store

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.100
all of our data.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:53.490
So Cloud Firestore is
a database in the Cloud

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.755
that is built as part
of the Firebase service.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:01.640
It is divided into documents.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.330
So if you are familiar
with a NoSQL model--

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.940
maybe you've used the real-time
time database before--

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:09.980
this is similar but
has some benefits.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:13.480
So a document has inside
of it key value pairs

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.310
that we're calling fields.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.980
Keys are strings,
and values can be

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:19.990
all sorts of different things--

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.160
strings, numbers, binary values.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:30.683
They could be Booleans, and even
these objecty-looking things.

00:08:30.683 --> 00:08:32.350
So you have all sorts
of things that you

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.745
can put inside of a document.

00:08:35.745 --> 00:08:37.890
Documents are stored
in collections,

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:41.450
which are, of course,
collections of documents.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:46.400
And these documents can point to
other collections of documents.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.900
You cannot have a document
inside of a document,

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.390
but you can have
a sub-collection.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:54.337
So what you end up with is a
structure somewhat like this.

00:08:54.337 --> 00:08:56.670
So what is the advantage of
storing your data like this,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.820
as opposed to just a full
Jason blob like you might

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:02.870
get in the real-time database?

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:06.550
What this allows you to
do is make shallow queries

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:07.240
for one thing.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.700
So let's say, I just
want the information

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:12.940
that's in that highlighted box.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.970
In a NoSQL model sometimes,
if you want to query that,

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.580
you're going to get all of
the information underneath it.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:22.400
So you really have to design
your database structure,

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.840
such that you keep it
as shallow as possible.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.590
And that means that
it doesn't necessarily

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:29.082
match with your application.

00:09:29.082 --> 00:09:30.790
This allows you to
make a data model that

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:32.860
really mirrors your
application and makes

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:34.420
it a lot easier to understand.

00:09:37.183 --> 00:09:38.850
So on the back-end,
Cloud Firebase Store

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.140
supports security rules.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.630
So along with
Firebase Auth, they

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.120
can help you make sure that
users only access the data

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.410
that you want them to access.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:51.960
So this is where Auth and Cloud
Firestore work nicely together.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.110
Since we can identify our
users by their user ID,

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.230
we can make sure that
securely, they can only

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:02.520
check their own data, and we are
able to look at any scheduling

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.730
data.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:07.250
So the final technology
when we want to talk about

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:09.892
is in terms of managing
customer payments.

00:10:09.892 --> 00:10:11.600
And this is the bulk
of what you're going

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.080
to see us working on today.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.360
We are going to use Cloud
Functions for Firebase, which

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.700
are event-driven-- meaning
they react to events

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.300
that happen in your app.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:26.440
This could be when a user first
slides into Firebase Auth,

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.380
or writes to a particular
document in Cloud Firestore,

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.690
writes to a field in
the real-time database,

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:35.320
maybe triggers a
conversion analytics

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.760
event in Google Analytics.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:38.927
There are all sorts of
things that can trigger this.

00:10:38.927 --> 00:10:41.560
You could even have an
HTTP triggered function,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:45.400
so that you can trigger
it whatever you want to.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.580
Then, wherever
these events occur,

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.302
the Cloud Function
starts to run.

00:10:50.302 --> 00:10:51.760
And whatever code
that you have put

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:55.930
in it, whatever actions you want
to occur are going to happen.

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:58.540
Cloud Functions are written
in TypeScript or JavaScript

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:03.130
on top of No.js, which means you
have the option to incorporate

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.800
all sorts of NPM packages.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.770
We're looking at 350,000
different packages

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:09.740
at your disposal.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.500
So you can definitely
customize it.

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:14.550
This is not just
for Google products.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.050
You can incorporate all of the
different APIs that you need,

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:21.330
which we'll see today in
terms of using the Stripe API.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.760
So Cloud Functions work inside
a privileged Google environment,

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.790
which means that
unlike the client code,

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:28.890
they aren't going to be
subject to security rules.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:30.270
This is really
beneficial for when

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.103
you want to make actions
that you don't want

00:11:32.103 --> 00:11:34.350
to be accessible to the client.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:36.420
You can send things
here and do checks

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.980
before writing to
places in the database,

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.980
for instance, that you
wouldn't want your client

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.410
to be able to do so.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:49.970
And if you are using any sort
of Google Cloud Services,

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:51.542
usually setup is a snap.

00:11:51.542 --> 00:11:53.000
And you don't even
necessarily need

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.000
to include some of those
configuration steps

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.460
that you're used to setting up.

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:01.740
So now that you know about why
we're using these technologies,

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:04.413
let's dig into the good
stuff, the payments.

00:12:04.413 --> 00:12:05.330
SUSAN GOLDBLATT: Cool.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.630
So we are going to do another
four-step process to manage

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:09.800
our customer payments.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.050
The first step in there is
creating a Stripe customer.

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:15.710
So we know through Firebase
Auth that we have a customer.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:17.900
But we need to let Stripe
know because that way,

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:19.790
we can actually do
things like add cards,

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:23.360
and also charge payments,
and make refunds.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.420
So that's step 1.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.670
Step 2 is then creating
a payment method.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:28.170
So sometimes, we
say payment method.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:29.570
Sometimes, we say add a card.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.280
And sometimes, we say source.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.620
It's just a way of
paying for the service.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.120
So we'll do that.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.540
And then next, we
make the payment, so

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.860
processing the credit card.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.650
Then finally, we'll
handle the refund.

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:49.230
So let's go into the first step
of creating a Stripe customer.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.660
The way that this
will work is the user

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:53.990
will log in and create
a Firebase Auth user.

00:12:56.418 --> 00:12:58.960
From there, we'll go ahead and
trigger a Cloud Function based

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:02.540
on the creation of that user.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.890
Then, we'll talk to the Stripe
SDK and wait for the data

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:10.130
to come back in
the Cloud Function.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:13.010
Then, with the data back, we'll
write that to Cloud Firestore,

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:16.390
so that we can use it later.

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:18.640
Let's look at what this is
going to look like in code.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:20.900
So let me walk
through some of this.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:22.600
It's a lot of code
on the screen.

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:26.190
So here we have a function
called Create Stripe Customer.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.140
The function is
triggered off of an auth.

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.730
So you can see the functions
that auth that user.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.090
And then, on Create--
so that means that when

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.800
a user is created,
we'll run this function.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:37.290
Then, we get the
user object here.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.390
So that contains
information that we'll

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:41.640
use like the email
address or other things

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:42.610
that we might need.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:46.710
So what we do is just talk
to the Stripe API here.

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.390
So Stripe.customers.create.

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:50.310
So we're saying, hey,
Stripe, make a customer.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:51.562
Here's their email address.

00:13:51.562 --> 00:13:53.520
Super easy, and then we
just wait for that data

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:54.390
to come back.

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:56.820
And we use a wait here because
it's TypeScript JavaScript.

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:59.237
And if we don't use a wait,
it'll just fall right through,

00:13:59.237 --> 00:14:01.350
and we won't get the data back.

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:02.792
So we have to wait.

00:14:02.792 --> 00:14:04.000
And we wait for the customer.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.730
And then, with that
customer, we store it

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.780
back to Cloud Firestore.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:11.170
So we do that by saying
admin.firestore.collection.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:13.630
And then, we have a collection
called Stripe Customers.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:16.180
And that collection is
made up of all of our users

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.440
with the user.uid.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:21.160
So we'll see in Firestore
this collection and then

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:22.088
a bunch of documents.

00:14:22.088 --> 00:14:24.130
And each of those documents
will have data that's

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:25.630
related to their Stripe ID.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:28.640
And then, we'll also add
other information there.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:30.140
So let's look at
what this will look

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:32.867
like in our actual application.

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:39.870
So here you can see, we go
and click the Login button.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:43.190
And this will pop up some
really nice UI from Firebase.

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:45.210
And this is all part
of authentication.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:49.310
So we'll go into the actual
Google account to log in.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.860
We pick our login account
that we want to use.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:56.040
And we can see here, we've
logged in successfully.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.440
If we look at our Data
Viewer, the Stripe customer

00:14:58.440 --> 00:14:59.640
is added there.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.880
And when we click into it,
we have the customer ID

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:03.088
that we got back from Stripe.

00:15:06.743 --> 00:15:08.410
Cool, so I'll pass
it off to Jen to talk

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:12.160
about how we're going to
create a Stripe payment method.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.240
JENNIFER PERSON: So now that
we have a Stripe customer

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.700
associated with a
Firebase user, we

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:19.770
want to be able to
add a method for them

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:20.895
to pay for our services.

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.600
This involves the
Client Stripe SDK,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.700
creating what is called a token.

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:32.820
A token is a single-use
payment method.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:36.650
So you give, say, a credit
card or some other source

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:40.010
of information to the
Stripe Client SDK,

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:41.810
and they give you
back this token

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:44.820
that they identify
as the credit card.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:47.410
So it doesn't actually
have specific information

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:49.910
about the credit card in it--
which is nice because then, we

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:55.000
can write it to Cloud Firestore
without actually storing any

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:58.210
of that very sensitive data.

00:15:58.210 --> 00:15:59.710
At every point in
this, you will see

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:02.890
that we don't store anything
that is identifiable in terms

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:05.290
of someone being able
to take the credit card

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:06.940
information from a user.

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:10.990
So we want to store stuff
that even if it leaked

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:12.500
wouldn't have much of an impact.

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:14.080
So in this case,
we're just going

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:18.400
to write that one-time token,
which is naturally going

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:19.605
to trigger a Cloud Function.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:26.130
At this point, we're going to
speak to the Stripe Server SDK,

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:29.010
and take that token
and our customer ID,

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:30.630
and link them
together, which gives

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:32.490
us what's called a Source.

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:34.470
a Source and a token
are very similar,

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:36.840
but a source is something
that you can use over and over

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.440
because we think that once
someone orders flowers

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.190
through Mobile Florist,
they're definitely

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:44.940
going to want to do so again.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:48.180
Finally, we're going to write
information about the Source

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:50.970
into Cloud Firestore,
which enables the client

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.910
to be able to see that the
card was added successfully

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:57.770
and also see some
information about their card.

00:16:57.770 --> 00:17:02.480
So here's what that Cloud
Function looks like in code.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.030
Our function is called
Add Payment Source.

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:07.970
And in terms of its
form, we have functions

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:12.800
again dot Firestore instead
of in the previous case,

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:13.910
you saw off.

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:17.119
Here we have a document which
is Stripe customer's user

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:19.190
ID, tokens, auto ID.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.200
This is where that
token is being written.

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.060
You'll see that a
couple of these fields

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.460
are in brackets, which means
that they are wild cards.

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.195
So whatever it happens
to be in that place

00:17:28.195 --> 00:17:29.570
will still trigger
this function.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:31.760
It doesn't need to
actually say user ID.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.170
In this case, it's
actually going

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:37.550
to be the Firebase user's UID.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.710
This isn't on write
function, which

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:41.330
means it is triggered
whenever there

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:43.850
is a write to that
specific location.

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:45.590
So that could be
when it is first

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:49.033
created, when a change is made,
when something is deleted--

00:17:49.033 --> 00:17:51.200
all of that is going to
trigger this function, which

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:53.783
could be beneficial and works
for this particular situation.

00:17:53.783 --> 00:17:55.700
But you'll see that there
are some cases where

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:57.650
you don't want that.

00:17:57.650 --> 00:18:01.040
So to get the data
from Firestore,

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.010
we're going to call
change.after.data.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:06.790
And this is going to
give us what was just

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:09.070
changed in Cloud Firestore.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.860
If there is no data because
it was just a delete,

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:13.940
we're just going to return
out of this function.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:15.160
We don't need to proceed.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:18.550
Otherwise, we're going to
get the token from that data.

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:23.650
And then, we are going to
get the Stripe customer

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:28.210
ID from Cloud Firestore.

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:31.902
So this is what we stored in
the first function that you saw.

00:18:31.902 --> 00:18:33.610
And this is going to
come up a lot, where

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:35.277
we're going to need
to get that customer

00:18:35.277 --> 00:18:38.163
ID based on the Firebase user.

00:18:38.163 --> 00:18:40.330
So we're just going to put
that in its own function,

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:42.100
and you'll see get customer ID.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:44.380
And that means we're
going to get the customer

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:47.230
ID for this particular
customer, which enables

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:49.300
us to do something like this.

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.310
CallStripe.customers
create source,

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:54.840
pass the customer,
end of the token.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.210
And then, we're going to
write that information back

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:58.058
to the database.

00:18:58.058 --> 00:18:59.850
Notice, we also have
something that instead

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.010
of just a random ID
for the document,

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:03.600
we're using something
called Response.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.710
In the Response, we
have fingerprint,

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:08.430
which is a unique
identifier of that source.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:12.430
And that actually just makes
it easy to query this later.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.010
We also call merge true, so
that rather than overriding

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:16.510
all the information
in the document,

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:18.040
it just merges the
fields together.

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:24.406
So let's see what this
looks like in action.

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:33.062
Here, you'll see
adding a credit card.

00:19:33.062 --> 00:19:34.270
Feel free to try to use this.

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:35.340
It's just a Stripe test card.

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:37.020
It's really not
going to do anything.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:38.310
Also, it's expired.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.680
So it's really not good.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:41.190
Good luck.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:44.610
So the card has been saved.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:46.590
You'll see it says Success.

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:52.207
And after a moment, the UI
updates to show the new card.

00:19:52.207 --> 00:19:54.290
So here, I can show a
little information about it.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:58.400
And then, in Cloud
Firestore, you

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:00.933
can see the kind of
information that comes back.

00:20:00.933 --> 00:20:01.850
A lot of these are No.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.230
But if you wanted to provide
that information as well,

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:04.730
you could.

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:13.040
OK, so we have our
Stripe customer.

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:15.180
We have their payment
method all cued up.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.242
Let's move on to
actually making a payment

00:20:17.242 --> 00:20:18.950
because that's the
part I'm excited about

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:20.638
is making some money.

00:20:20.638 --> 00:20:21.680
SUSAN GOLDBLATT: Awesome.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:24.080
Same, same.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.270
So the way that this
will work is a client

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:28.757
will write to Cloud Firestore
the charge that they're

00:20:28.757 --> 00:20:29.340
going to make.

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.760
So that'll come from the app,
and we'll see that in our video

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:33.260
later.

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:37.040
But we'll write to
Cloud Firestore.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.110
Then after that, we'll
trigger a Cloud Function.

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:43.190
So hopefully, this is starting
to sound a little familiar.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:46.910
Then, our Cloud Function
will talk to the Stripe SDK.

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:49.970
And we'll wait for
that data to come back.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:52.020
Then, with that
information, we'll

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:55.850
return the result
to Cloud Firestore.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:57.600
So before I go into
the code for this,

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.642
I want to talk a little
bit about Cloud Functions

00:20:59.642 --> 00:21:00.560
for Firebase Retries.

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:02.530
So Cloud Functions for
Firebase are guaranteed

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:05.000
to run at least once,
but that means that they

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:06.380
could run more than once.

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:09.020
And we need our function
to be able to support that.

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.400
This is really important
when you're making payments

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:14.720
because if you run a function
that charges a customer more

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:17.150
than once, it could charge
them more than once--

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:19.110
which would be very bad.

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:20.600
And users would be writing in.

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:22.200
They wouldn't be using our app.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.950
So we really want to make sure
that we handle this correctly.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:26.700
So the way that we're
going to handle this

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:28.220
is called Idempotency.

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:30.860
It's a concept that if you
run something more than once

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:37.050
with the same arguments, only
one side effect of it will run.

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:41.720
So in this case, we're going
to talk to the Stripe API.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:44.990
And if the Cloud
Function retries,

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:47.720
the Stripe API knows
with our Idempotency key

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:49.880
that we only want
to charge one time.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:51.710
So we give them the
same Idempotency key,

00:21:51.710 --> 00:21:55.318
and they won't recharge
the customer every time.

00:21:55.318 --> 00:21:57.360
So we'll see this in the
code a little bit later.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:01.310
But just wanted to set
the stage for that.

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:03.960
So let's look at the code.

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:06.090
This function is called
Create Stripe Customer.

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:09.828
So it's run off of
functions.Firestore.document.

00:22:09.828 --> 00:22:12.120
And that's when this is
created, so whenever a document

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:14.190
is created in this location.

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:16.680
So we're in the collections
write customers.

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:20.250
We have our user ID, so
whatever user we're in.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.080
And then, we have a
sub-collection charges.

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.660
And then, the charge
will get an auto ID

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:29.697
that is randomly generated
but unique by Firestore.

00:22:29.697 --> 00:22:31.030
And we get the snap and context.

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:33.100
So we can get information
from that as well.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:35.538
So first thing that we
do is get the customer ID

00:22:35.538 --> 00:22:37.080
since we need that
to talk to Stripe.

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:40.860
We'll use our Get Customer
ID function here and pass

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.540
in the context.params.userID.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:47.630
Next, we get the amount
that they're being charged.

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:50.930
So snap.data.amount will
have that information.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:52.730
And then, we create
a charge object,

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:57.107
which is just the amount, and
the currency, and the customer.

00:22:57.107 --> 00:22:58.440
Next, we need to set the source.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:00.480
So what credit card are
they going to be using?

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:01.670
Do they want to
use a default card?

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:03.087
Or are they using
one that they've

00:23:03.087 --> 00:23:04.177
put in for the first time?

00:23:04.177 --> 00:23:06.260
Maybe this is a corporate
account for some reason,

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:09.530
and they have a
different credit card.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:12.110
So after that, we need to
get our Idempotency key.

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:14.630
So what are we going
to use for that here?

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:19.490
Well, it needs to be unique,
and it needs to be not changing,

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:21.560
even though if we run
this more than once.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:25.830
So a good example here would
be the auto ID of our document.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:29.420
So even if this function
runs more than once,

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:30.810
the auto ID won't change.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:32.840
So it makes a really
good Idempotency key,

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:38.270
and so we'll just grab that
from the context.params.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:41.480
Then, we'll talk to Stripe
and pass in our charge

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:44.180
that we created earlier with
our currency, and amount,

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:48.450
and customer, and also give
it the Idempotency key.

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:51.000
Then, we wait for that
data to come back.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:55.193
And then, store it
in Cloud Firestore.

00:23:55.193 --> 00:23:57.360
You'll notice here that we
wrap this in a try catch.

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.590
And that's because this
logic is really important.

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:02.130
And we want to make
sure any errors are

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:03.520
serviced to the user.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:08.040
So we have a function that
says User Facing Message.

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:09.800
And then, we put
the error in there,

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:11.550
so the user can
understand what's going on

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:14.680
with their application.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:17.353
So let's look at what this will
look like in our application.

00:24:17.353 --> 00:24:19.020
JENNIFER PERSON: Now,
I'd also just like

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:21.150
to note very briefly
that there is

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:24.060
the risk of the client pressing
the button more than once.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.250
Maybe they're just
really eager to pay you.

00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:28.830
And resolving that is actually
a lot easier than dealing

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:32.370
with Idempotency, which
is the button in this case

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:36.030
is no longer active until
a response comes back

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:39.417
from the Cloud Functions.

00:24:39.417 --> 00:24:42.000
SUSAN GOLDBLATT: So here we are
write who the flowers are for,

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:44.530
click Pay, Payment
Was Successful.

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:47.550
We can wait for that
data to come back,

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:49.830
and we see the
charge to Puf here.

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:51.930
We go and look into our charges.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:54.340
We can see all of this
information back from Stripe

00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:54.840
here.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:56.820
So we were able to
successfully charge

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.305
our credit card from Stripe.

00:24:59.305 --> 00:25:01.680
And we get all this information
back that can be helpful.

00:25:04.498 --> 00:25:06.540
Now, I'll pass it off to
Jen to talk a little bit

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:09.930
about handling refunds because
sometimes, the flowers aren't

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:12.052
always the freshest.

00:25:12.052 --> 00:25:13.510
JENNIFER PERSON:
Right, so we would

00:25:13.510 --> 00:25:15.580
like to think that
we will never have

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:17.950
to refund our customers
because their product is just

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:18.640
that great.

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:21.610
But quite frankly, it is going
to come up from time to time.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.400
So in our case,
we want to set up

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:27.310
a system where users can
request a refund if they

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:28.900
think that one is deserved.

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:31.060
But we want to be able
to have the final say,

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:32.830
and what we are
actually going to refund

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:36.180
to people because there
are certainly cases where

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:37.530
a refund would be a good idea.

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:40.810
Maybe they ended up with black
roses instead of red roses.

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:44.500
But there are some cases
where it's really not on us.

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:46.050
So if they send
flowers to someone,

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:48.500
but that person doesn't
actually want them.

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:51.310
So we want to be able
to pick and choose which

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.720
refunds we want to approve.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.770
So in order to see how we're
going to pick and choose that,

00:25:56.770 --> 00:26:00.220
let's look at how we are
going to approve refunds.

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:01.630
Starting to look familiar?

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:04.030
The client is going to
write a refund request

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:07.390
to Cloud Firestore, which is
going to end up in a queue

00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:09.100
that we can see.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:13.000
If we decide that it is
a reasonable request,

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:17.380
we will approve that refund.

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:19.090
This approval is
going to trigger

00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:21.310
a Cloud Function,
which is actually

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:25.560
going to do the refunding.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:30.690
So we need a way to distinguish
between moderators--

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:32.902
which right now is just us,
but as our company grows,

00:26:32.902 --> 00:26:34.110
there could be more of them--

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:37.470
and regular customers,
so that users

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:40.450
can't just approve
their own refunds.

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:42.820
The way that we're going to
do that is through a concept

00:26:42.820 --> 00:26:45.750
called Custom Claims.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:48.415
In Firebase Auth,
when you sign in,

00:26:48.415 --> 00:26:51.080
a user gives you a whole lot
of different information,

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:53.830
including the user
ID, their email, what

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:55.480
providers they've
signed in with,

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:59.620
maybe an image URL, which could
have come from their provider

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:01.600
or could be provided by them.

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:05.470
And then, they can
upload one, and then you

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:06.400
could update the user.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:08.930
There's all sorts of
information that's in there.

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:11.260
There's also this little
bit of information

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:15.120
on their Auth token,
which are Custom Claims.

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:19.870
And these can be any kind of
key value pairs that you want.

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:22.240
But do note that they have
a very low limit in size.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:25.840
I think it's like
1,000 bytes, maybe.

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:28.840
It's really not meant for those
cases where you're like, hey,

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:32.050
it would be great if I could put
it in their whole home address

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:35.590
into the user or the
list of their friends.

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:38.260
It's just for little
pieces of information

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:41.390
that can distinguish one
kind of user from another.

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:44.170
So in this case, we're
going to add to our token

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:47.570
a claim called moderator, and
we're going to set it to true.

00:27:47.570 --> 00:27:49.210
So this is how
we're going to see,

00:27:49.210 --> 00:27:51.200
does this claim exist on a user.

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:56.380
Then they have the right to make
changes such as giving refunds.

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:59.380
This also enables us to
make a different UI where

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:01.280
we can see more information.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.400
This allows us to
distinguish between us

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:07.600
being able to see every
scheduled delivery and clients

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:10.750
just being able to
see their deliveries.

00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:13.990
So how do we make sure that only
authorized folks can actually

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:16.270
become moderators?

00:28:16.270 --> 00:28:19.990
We don't want someone to just
be able to say, I'm a moderator.

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:22.310
Give me that claim.

00:28:22.310 --> 00:28:26.410
So what we're going
to do is make it

00:28:26.410 --> 00:28:30.460
so that only existing moderators
can give that privilege.

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:33.860
And in this case, you can get
more hierarchical if you want.

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:35.890
Maybe you could have
an owner property

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:38.110
and only that person
gets to grant privileges

00:28:38.110 --> 00:28:39.160
to other people.

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:40.750
For simplicity's
sake, we are just

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:43.570
checking for a single
claim, but remember

00:28:43.570 --> 00:28:47.723
that you can do sort of
a combination of them.

00:28:47.723 --> 00:28:49.140
So this is what
the function looks

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:52.470
like to give that permission.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:56.370
We are using an HTTP
function, but this one

00:28:56.370 --> 00:28:57.370
is a little different.

00:28:57.370 --> 00:29:01.620
It is an onCall function,
which means this call can only

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:06.570
be made from a Firebase client,
your Android app or your iOS

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:11.000
app, rather than just being
able to make it from anywhere.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:14.180
In this case, passes
data and context.

00:29:14.180 --> 00:29:17.590
We check the context
because it also

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:21.370
passes information about the
user who has made the request.

00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:23.650
We check their Auth token
and see if they are already

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:24.670
a moderator.

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:27.490
If they are not, we let them
know that they don't have

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:31.050
permission to make this change.

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:33.460
This format might
look a little strange

00:29:33.460 --> 00:29:36.440
if you're used to
doing HTTP calls.

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:38.380
But this is just how it's done.

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:40.660
So you can return
your fields instead

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:44.290
of doing a response.send.

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:46.270
If they are a
moderator, we're going

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:50.660
to get the email of the user
that they want to promote.

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:52.510
So in this case,
if I'm a moderator

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:54.010
and I want to promote
someone else--

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:55.240
I want to promote Susan--

00:29:55.240 --> 00:29:57.070
I would pass her email.

00:29:57.070 --> 00:29:58.560
If there's no email,
I'm just going

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.950
to let them know, just keeping
all my checks in there.

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:06.820
Then we have a function
called getUserByEmail,

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:10.140
which enables us to get all
the information about the user

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:11.982
based on just their email.

00:30:11.982 --> 00:30:14.190
If there doesn't happen to
be a user with that email,

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:16.345
maybe Susan hasn't
signed up yet, again,

00:30:16.345 --> 00:30:17.970
we're just going to
have another check.

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:20.850
Otherwise, we're going to
get the user's UID, which

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:24.600
is what is required
to add these claims.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:29.360
So then we set custom user
claims using their user ID

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.230
and then passing
whatever claims we want.

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:33.260
In this case, we're just
doing moderator true.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:35.630
You could do several
claims at once if you want.

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:39.952
You can do them over time, and
they'll still all be there.

00:30:39.952 --> 00:30:41.660
And then we're going
to return a response

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:43.860
that user is a moderator.

00:30:43.860 --> 00:30:46.400
So this is how we're
making sure that only we

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:51.080
can approve moderators and only
people with the moderator claim

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:56.150
are going to be able
to make refunds.

00:30:56.150 --> 00:30:59.180
So once a moderator
approves the refund,

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:02.060
it's still going to need
to be processed by Stripe.

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:04.285
So Susan is going to
show you how to do that.

00:31:04.285 --> 00:31:06.520
SUSAN GOLDBLATT: Awesome.

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:07.020
Cool.

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:10.117
So we have this refund request
that the user is written into.

00:31:10.117 --> 00:31:12.200
And I just wanted to give
you all a little insight

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:14.580
into what this might look like.

00:31:14.580 --> 00:31:16.310
So we have the amount,
the approvedAt,

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:19.520
which is what our moderator
would say yes or no to,

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.430
an ID, closedAt, all
this type of information.

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:25.100
I'm just trying to add more
dates to these so that we

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:28.400
can have an insight if something
goes wrong with the customer

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:31.070
to look back and be like, oh,
it was closed at this time.

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:32.400
It was approved at this time.

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:36.410
So this is just a little
insight into how we're

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:39.760
structuring some of our data.

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:46.650
So let's go back to our example
of how the approvals process

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:48.820
for refunds get processed.

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:52.530
So we write to Cloud Firestore
to request the approval,

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:56.490
and then a moderator will
come in and approve it or not.

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.500
Then that moderator
coming in to approve

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:00.900
will trigger a Cloud Function.

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:04.440
And then our Cloud Function
will talk to the Stripe SDK,

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:07.500
and then we will write that
data back to Cloud Firestore

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:08.380
after that.

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:11.133
So that's kind of the process.

00:32:11.133 --> 00:32:12.550
And so now we have
our moderators,

00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:14.133
so we have a way to
create moderators,

00:32:14.133 --> 00:32:17.820
and we need to look at the code
to actually talk to Stripe.

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:19.887
So let's look at that.

00:32:19.887 --> 00:32:21.720
Here we have a function
called refundCharge,

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:23.760
and so this is based
off a function,

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:27.000
that firestore.document--
so a refund request--

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:28.460
and then on the ID.

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:30.210
And you can see this
is onUpdate function,

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:33.360
so this will run when
there's an update.

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:35.460
And we'll get the
change in the context

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:37.038
here too for this function.

00:32:37.038 --> 00:32:38.580
So the first thing
that we want to do

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:42.990
is make sure that the
user is a moderator.

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:45.090
And if they're not
a moderator, we

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:46.660
want to just return an error.

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:48.790
And I've included an
attemptedAt here to be like,

00:32:48.790 --> 00:32:50.070
hey, someone tried
to refund this,

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:51.280
and they weren't a moderator.

00:32:51.280 --> 00:32:53.900
So maybe pay attention to that
if you're looking through it.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:59.950
After that, we want to do a
little bit more error checking

00:32:59.950 --> 00:33:00.450
here too.

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:03.380
So we get our refund that
we're trying to issue,

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:07.160
and then we also want to
get a refund object before.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:09.230
So we get our refund that
we're trying to issue

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:10.920
by saying, change.after.data.

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:14.403
So that would be the data after
the change has been applied.

00:33:14.403 --> 00:33:16.820
And we can get the data before
the change has been applied

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:20.050
by saying, change.before.data.

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:22.400
So we want to make
sure two things here.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:27.250
One, that the refund hasn't
already been approved and two,

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:31.203
that the previous refund
hasn't been approved.

00:33:31.203 --> 00:33:32.620
So we want to make
sure you're not

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:35.078
trying to re-refund something
that's already been approved.

00:33:35.078 --> 00:33:38.750
And then the second one is that
there is an approval on it.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:42.580
So make sure that it actually
gets approved by someone.

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:44.080
If either of those
are fall through,

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:45.830
so if no one if it's
already been approved

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:49.940
or if no one approved it, also
set an attemptedAt and return.

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:51.450
So don't process this refund.

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:57.170
So now with all of that
error checking aside,

00:33:57.170 --> 00:33:59.300
we're going to go ahead
and talk to Stripe

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:00.930
to process the refund.

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:05.570
So we call stripe.refunds.create
with our charge and the refund

00:34:05.570 --> 00:34:06.580
ID.

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:09.060
And we wait for that
data to come back.

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:13.469
Here, the response can succeed,
and so if it's succeeded,

00:34:13.469 --> 00:34:17.250
we'll say, great, refund it at
this time and return that back.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:19.679
And we'll let the user know.

00:34:19.679 --> 00:34:22.739
But if the refund
doesn't succeed,

00:34:22.739 --> 00:34:26.429
we also need to make sure
that we set that data as well.

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:28.440
So we'll say, it was
attempted at this time

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:32.310
and send the data back to the
customer and to the moderator

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:35.159
so they can look through it.

00:34:35.159 --> 00:34:37.230
Finally, we wrapped all
of this in a try catch.

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:39.480
So just in case there
was an error talking

00:34:39.480 --> 00:34:41.730
to Stripe for some
reason that wasn't

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:44.280
a successful response or
an unsuccessful response,

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:45.880
we also want to
keep track of that.

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:49.739
So we'll say, hey, you
attempted this at this time,

00:34:49.739 --> 00:34:52.449
and it didn't work.

00:34:52.449 --> 00:34:53.610
And then report the error.

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:55.560
So we keep track of
all of that in order

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:59.460
to just help understand
the process for the refunds

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:03.360
and understand where our
users are in the process.

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:09.450
So let's look at this
in our application.

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:12.500
So here, we have our list
of approvals over there,

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.900
and we can see approvedAt.

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:16.640
So it was approved
by our moderator,

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:19.310
and then this all has the data
about when it was refunded,

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:22.260
the response that we got
back, stuff like that.

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:25.970
So you can see that process
of going through and approving

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:27.200
things.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:29.050
So the next step
two, we just covered

00:35:29.050 --> 00:35:32.320
a bunch of data protection
or these if cases,

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:32.990
stuff like that.

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:36.070
We also want to make sure, on
top of our moderator, that we

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:37.610
want to protect this data.

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:40.630
And so as we mentioned
earlier, Cloud Firestore

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:43.180
has rules when it's
talking with the client

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:45.520
to keep things secure.

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:47.860
So let's write some
rules specifically

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:54.458
for refunds that can cover
some of our security concerns.

00:35:54.458 --> 00:35:56.000
So if you're not
familiar with rules,

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:57.720
it's a match-based statement.

00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:01.475
So here we have at the top,
our databases and the database

00:36:01.475 --> 00:36:03.350
and the documents that
we're trying to match.

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:07.010
And then each path in the
database gets kind of-- we

00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:08.310
can give it its own rules.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.630
So this particular statement
is for the refund request

00:36:11.630 --> 00:36:12.680
collection.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:14.480
And the refund ID
there is a wildcard,

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:18.370
so it matches to any of the
refund IDs that you pass in.

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:20.040
So these are all
ordered together,

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:23.520
and so you'll have more
complex rules as you build out

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:25.020
your Firestore database.

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:28.940
But this is just, as I said,
the refund request ones.

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:32.490
So what we're allowing here
is, allow read and create.

00:36:32.490 --> 00:36:35.360
So if the user is
logged in and they're

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:38.150
the one trying to
create the refund

00:36:38.150 --> 00:36:40.430
and they're not
including approved that--

00:36:40.430 --> 00:36:44.780
so approve that is not in
the request.resource.data,

00:36:44.780 --> 00:36:46.370
they can read and create.

00:36:46.370 --> 00:36:48.420
So they can't
approve themselves.

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:50.930
So this rule prevents that.

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:52.820
Then the next rule,
allow read and write,

00:36:52.820 --> 00:36:56.660
is so that moderators-- so you
can actually access that custom

00:36:56.660 --> 00:36:57.990
claim token here.

00:36:57.990 --> 00:37:00.980
So if
request.auth.token.moderator is

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:02.590
true--

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:04.610
sorry, that was a lot of words.

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:07.217
But if that's true,
you can read and write.

00:37:07.217 --> 00:37:09.800
So that allows our moderators
to go in and update the request.

00:37:13.040 --> 00:37:15.200
So with that, I'll
pass it off to Jenn

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:17.590
to talk a little bit
about our fifth topic.

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:19.520
JENNIFER PERSON: Yeah.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:22.460
So the first time we were
going to deliver this talk,

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:24.840
we actually had a
25 minute window.

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:27.230
So I'm super excited that
we have a little more room

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:29.900
because we're able to cover
some topics that I think are

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:32.120
very important and exciting.

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:36.420
And in this case, we're going
to be talking about webhooks.

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:40.950
So most payment
processors are going

00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:44.040
to offer you some kind
of webhook, an ability

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:50.160
to trigger an endpoint
when something happens

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:52.440
within the payment processor.

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:56.130
So in terms of Stripe, this
could be pretty much anything.

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:58.620
This could be when a
payment goes through,

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:00.960
when a payment is declined,
when a refund happens,

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:04.890
when a new customer is created,
when a new card is created.

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:08.760
They have a whole list of things
that you can trigger endpoints

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:10.660
from.

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:13.570
So again, expired credit cards,
failed charges, and stuff.

00:38:13.570 --> 00:38:16.290
So in terms of making
sure that it's just

00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:19.680
not some malicious person
triggering that endpoint,

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.930
Stripe offers you
a signature header

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:25.590
that you can use to verify
that this webhook actually

00:38:25.590 --> 00:38:31.090
came from Stripe and
not somewhere else.

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:36.910
So let's take a look at
one example of an endpoint.

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:46.270
In this case, we want to do
this whenever a customer makes

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:47.420
an update to their charge.

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:50.660
So in this case, if it seems
to be approved upfront,

00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:52.250
but then later it
actually fails,

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:55.240
we want to be able to keep
data in sync in Cloud Firestore

00:38:55.240 --> 00:38:57.320
to reflect that change.

00:38:57.320 --> 00:39:02.590
So this is a regular
HTTPS request, as opposed

00:39:02.590 --> 00:39:07.060
to the ones that are only called
from a Firebase client app.

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:08.920
So we're going to get the data.

00:39:08.920 --> 00:39:10.390
This is provided by Stripe.

00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:12.557
They're going to tell us
some information about what

00:39:12.557 --> 00:39:13.270
has happened.

00:39:13.270 --> 00:39:16.330
We're also going to get the
customer ID because that's

00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:17.710
provided by Stripe.

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:19.870
And then we're going
to get an ID, which

00:39:19.870 --> 00:39:22.915
is the ID of the charge
where that change happened.

00:39:26.105 --> 00:39:28.230
Now we're going to get the
Firebase user associated

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:29.490
with the Stripe customer ID.

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:33.270
So you've seen examples of where
we start with a Firebase user

00:39:33.270 --> 00:39:35.280
and then we get the
Stripe customer by looking

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.170
in the Cloud Firestore.

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:38.903
We can do the reverse as well.

00:39:38.903 --> 00:39:40.320
So in this case,
we're going to do

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:44.430
it using a query of
Firestore Stripe customers

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:47.490
where the customer ID
field of that document

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:49.770
is equal to the
customer ID field that

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:52.680
just came to us from Stripe.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:55.620
This does return an
array of documents

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:57.840
even if you set
the limit to one.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:00.300
So in this case, I
get the first document

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:04.140
which is going to be
our Firebase user who

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:06.210
has that customer ID.

00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.940
And then we're going to get
the reference to that path

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:13.140
to their customer path.

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:19.020
Similarly, we want to know
which of their charges something

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:19.560
happened to.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:21.300
So now that we know
which user it is,

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:27.510
we can go into their charges
and get the specific document

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:30.300
where the ID of the charge is
equal to the ID that was just

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:32.710
provided from Stripe.

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:35.440
Now, we are going to
add the information that

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:39.130
changed in that charge
to Cloud Firestore.

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:43.090
So in this case,
in that path, I'll

00:40:43.090 --> 00:40:46.180
set all of the data that
just came from Stripe

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:47.313
and again merge true.

00:40:47.313 --> 00:40:49.480
So we have the previous
information about the charge

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:53.050
and then any updates like the
fact that maybe it just failed.

00:40:53.050 --> 00:40:54.790
We also send a
response back to Stripe

00:40:54.790 --> 00:40:56.260
because they are
expecting to know

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:59.367
that something was successful.

00:40:59.367 --> 00:41:01.450
So you can imagine that
this could be very useful.

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:04.290
You could have a specific
endpoint for all sorts

00:41:04.290 --> 00:41:05.490
of events that happen.

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:11.170
So today, we talked to
you about a whole lot.

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:14.680
We threw a whole lot at
you at once, including

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:18.040
how to create a Stripe customer
and link it to a Firebase user,

00:41:18.040 --> 00:41:20.410
how to store information
about a straight payment

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:23.230
method in Cloud
Firestore without storing

00:41:23.230 --> 00:41:26.920
the specific information that
makes that card identifiable,

00:41:26.920 --> 00:41:30.370
how to pay for things by
writing the Cloud Firestore

00:41:30.370 --> 00:41:33.310
and handling the payments
from the Stripe server API.

00:41:33.310 --> 00:41:35.380
Now certainly, you can
pay from the client,

00:41:35.380 --> 00:41:36.910
but the benefit of
doing it this way

00:41:36.910 --> 00:41:38.920
is that you are then a lot--

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:41.440
it makes it much easier
to function and update

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:42.340
Cloud Firestore.

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:45.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

